  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nIssued 354 AM MDT MON OCT 19 2026\nThe low pressure system will move NE over the area through the \nday Thursday into Friday. Shower and thunderstorm chances will \nbe greatest during this time, especially as it moves overhead. \nBroad cloud cover will limit instability, but the colder \natmospheric mid-levels (with still warm surface temperatures) \nwill enable a 10-20% chance of thunderstorms. Clouds and \nprecipitation will keep temperatures near normal on Thursday, \nwith widespread precipitation chances 50-90%. Precipitation \nchances will gradually clear west-to-east across the area Friday\nas a cold front moves through. This front will cool \ntemperatures to 5-10 degrees below normal Friday and bring wind \ngusts up of 30-40 mph out of the NW. The highest gusts will be \nseen along the ridgetops of SE Oregon and through western Snake \nPlain. Winds will remain strong through Friday evening. \nPrecipitation totals have been increasing over the past few \nmodel runs. Over the course of the event, rainfall accumulation \nis forecast at 0.1-0.3 inches in lower elevations, and 0.25-0.75\ninches for mountain valleys. Mountain summits and ridges could \nsee 0.5-1 inches of rainfall, and 1-4 inches of snow on summits\nabove 8000 ft MSL. Snow is unlikely on mountains until Friday \nmorning when the cold front drops snow levels from near 9000 ft \nMSL to 4500-6000 ft MSL (lowest in SE Oregon).\n\nTemperatures are forecast to be near normal Saturday through\nSunday, with another significant upper-level trough looking to \nimpact the area Sunday into Monday. Behind the earlier system, \nsnow levels will remain around 6500 ft MSL. Precipitation on \nSunday will fall as snow in mountain passes and the highest \nmountain towns if the temperature forecast holds steady. Also \nbehind the earlier trough, mountain valley cold pools could \nsupport fog/stratus formation and allow snowfall in the valleys \non Sunday and Monday.",
